  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nCurrent radar imagery indicates lake effect and diurnal showers and \nthunderstorms ongoing across Lake Superior and the central U.P. \nAreas in the central and eastern U.P. are likely to see brief, \nspotty rain showers and isolated rumbles of thunder continue this \nafternoon as instability remains over the area.\n\nSeasonably cool temperatures return while lake effect rain chances \ncontinue through Thursday. Precipitation chances will be primarily \nover the eastern U.P., as winds over the eastern lake remain \nnorthwesterly with some CAA while winds over the western arm shift \nto westerly. Rainfall totals are expected to remain under five \nhundreths of an inch for areas that are impacted by showers. \nInstability over the lake is expected to be more limited tomorrow, \nhowever, a rumble of thunder or two cannot be entirely ruled out. \nThursday night into Friday a surface low deepens and shifts eastward \nover the northern Plains, creating a tight gradient of meridionally-\noriented isobars which will result in strengthening southerly winds \nand WAA across the Great Lakes region. Temperatures rise into the \n60s across the U.P. Friday afternoon and remain well above average \n(by 8-10 degrees) overnight as a result of the WAA.\n\nTemperatures are expected to be well above average this weekend (by \n15-25 degrees). Highs are likely (~70% chance) to reach the 80s in \nthe interior west and lows will remain above 55 degrees overnight. \nUpper-level ridging and dewpoints in the 50s indicate that clear \nskies and dry weather are likely. Elevated fire weather conditions \nmay be reached over this period, as sustained southerly winds remain \nat 10-15kts through the weekend, gusting up to 35mph, while RHs drop \nto 40-50% for the daytime hours and fail to reach above 85% \novernight. Looking ahead to the early workweek, troughing moves into \nthe area at the surface and upper levels. This will cause winds to \nshift northerly, bringing temperatures back to near-average via CAA. \nThese northerly winds could also bring our next best chance of \nprecipitation if sufficient moisture is present over northeastern \nCanada.",
